{"id": "15250", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=15250", "title": "Huntly (Scotland)", "text": "Huntly is a town in Aberdeenshire in northeast Scotland; in bygone times it was more snappily known as \"Milton of Strathbogie\". With a population of 4600 in 2022, Huntly is nowadays a commuter town for Aberdeen, as it's on the railway. The main reason to visit is the ruined castle.\nRhynie, also described here, is sometimes the butt of comedy for its rural obscurity, yet was once the seat of Pictish kings. \nGet in.\nThe nearest airport is Aberdeen (). You could take a taxi from there to Dyce railway station to avoid doubling back through the city. \nHuntly has trains every two hours from Aberdeen, taking 55 min via Dyce (for airport) and Inverurie, and continuing west to Keith, Elgin, Forres, Nairn and Inverness (another 75 min).\n is east side of town. It has a staffed ticket office and machines, toilets and a waiting room. There's step-free access to both platforms. \nStagecoach North Bus 10 runs hourly from Aberdeen Union Square, taking almost two hours via Inverurie, and continuing to Keith, Elgin, Forres, Nairn and Inverness.\nGet around.\nYou can easily walk between Huntly village and castle but you need wheels for outlying sights.\nKEF Taxis are on +44 1466 700316.\nBuy.\nAsda at the A96 / A97 junction south edge of Huntly is open M-Sa 7AM-11PM, Su 7AM-10PM and has a filling station.\nFarmers Market is held in Huntly first Saturday of the month 9AM-1PM in Market Square.\nConnect.\nAs of July 2026, Huntly and its approach roads have 4G from O2, Three and Vodafone, and 5G from EE."}
